<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Default runtime permission grants for the bundled F-Droid store.

  F-Droid ships as a priv-app, so its install-time permissions are granted at
  scan time, and its privileged install path runs through the Privileged
  Extension (see privapp-permissions-fdroid.xml). Its *runtime* (dangerous)
  permissions are a separate matter and start denied — verified on device, where
  all nine of them read granted=false.

  Only one of those nine is granted here.

  POST_NOTIFICATIONS is the one that breaks the app's core job. F-Droid's
  RepoUpdateWorker checks the configured repos on a schedule and reports what it
  finds by posting a notification; with the permission denied it still runs, and
  still finds updates, and the user is never told. A store that cannot say "there
  is an update" is not doing the thing a store is for. The failure is also
  invisible — nothing errors, the work just completes silently — so it is not
  something a user could diagnose and fix in Settings themselves.

  It is not self-healing either. F-Droid targets SDK 30, and for a legacy-target
  app Android 13 shows the notification prompt when the app first creates a
  notification channel rather than at a moment the user connects to updates, so
  a dismissed prompt leaves updates permanently silent with no obvious way back.

  DELIBERATELY NOT GRANTED — the other eight:

    CAMERA                          add a repo by scanning its QR code
    ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION          )
    BLUETOOTH_SCAN, _CONNECT        ) "nearby swap" peer-to-peer app sharing
    READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE           )
    READ_MEDIA_IMAGES/AUDIO/VIDEO   ) local repos on removable media,
                                      install-from-file

  These back optional features, each of which prompts in-app at the point the
  user actually invokes it, so denying them by default costs a prompt rather
  than a broken feature. Camera, location and Bluetooth on a *preinstalled*
  store are also exactly the grants a user would object to finding pre-made on
  their behalf, and pre-granting them buys nothing until the feature is used.
  If someone later wants nearby-swap to work without prompts, that is a
  deliberate decision to add here, not an oversight to correct.

  MECHANISM NOTES — see default-permissions-fydroid-gms.xml for the full write-up.
  The one that bites:

      Grants are applied when a user is first provisioned, not retroactively.
      An existing profile only re-reads this directory when the
      permission-upgrade epoch changes, and our build fingerprint is pinned, so
      that no longer happens on its own. ADDING THIS FILE THEREFORE REQUIRES
      BUMPING FYDROID_DEFAULT_PERMISSIONS_EPOCH in
      frameworks/base/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/Settings.java
      (currently 1 -> 2, patch 0051), or already-provisioned devices keep the
      old state and only freshly flashed profiles pick this up.

  fixed="false" leaves the user free to revoke it in Settings. This is a
  default, not a lock.
-->
<exceptions>
  <exception package="org.fdroid.fdroid">
    <permission name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" fixed="false"/>
  </exception>
</exceptions>
